1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,036
A server talks in text.

2
00:00:02,586 --> 00:00:05,871
Logs, lists, configs. Far too much to read by hand.

3
00:00:06,421 --> 00:00:10,240
Today you steer it: redirect, pipe, search, count, and pack it all up.

4
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:12,786
First,

5
00:00:12,786 --> 00:00:14,602
save output in a file.

6
00:00:14,602 --> 00:00:17,576
Type: echo hello server > hello.txt.

7
00:00:17,576 --> 00:00:20,631
The arrow sends output into the file.

8
00:00:21,181 --> 00:00:26,280
Nothing printed on screen. It went into the file. Read it with: cat hello.txt.

9
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:29,107
One arrow replaces the file.

10
00:00:29,107 --> 00:00:31,221
Two arrows add to the end.

11
00:00:31,221 --> 00:00:34,149
Type: echo second line >> hello.txt.

12
00:00:34,149 --> 00:00:35,043
Two arrows.

13
00:00:35,593 --> 00:00:39,305
Check it. Type: cat hello.txt. Both lines are there.

14
00:00:39,855 --> 00:00:46,405
Errors travel on their own channel, number two. Type: ls nothere 2> errors.txt.

15
00:00:46,955 --> 00:00:51,499
The error went into the file, not the screen. Type: cat errors.txt.

16
00:00:53,549 --> 00:01:01,400
A pipe sends one command's output into the next command. Type: ls /etc | head -n 5.

17
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:05,491
Only the first five lines. Head read what L S produced.

18
00:01:06,041 --> 00:01:12,441
Now count how many entries there are. Type: ls /etc | wc -l.

19
00:01:12,991 --> 00:01:16,639
One number. Two small tools, joined. This is the Linux way.

20
00:01:17,689 --> 00:01:25,582
Pipes chain as far as you like. Type: find . -name "*.log" | wc -l.

21
00:01:26,132 --> 00:01:30,100
How many log files live under here? Four. One line answered it.

22
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:34,057
Here is an application log.

23
00:01:34,057 --> 00:01:35,823
Type: grep ERROR app.log.

24
00:01:35,823 --> 00:01:38,507
Grep prints only the lines that match.

25
00:01:39,057 --> 00:01:44,433
Two errors, and nothing else. Now count them. Type: grep -c ERROR app.log.

26
00:01:44,983 --> 00:01:50,274
Add line numbers. Type: grep -n ERROR app.log. Now you know where to look.

27
00:01:50,824 --> 00:01:56,712
Ignore upper and lower case with dash i. Type: grep -i warning app.log.

28
00:01:57,262 --> 00:02:03,875
Everything except a word. Dash v inverts the match. Type: grep -v INFO app.log.

29
00:02:04,425 --> 00:02:10,591
And search a whole folder. Dash r is recursive. Type: grep -r timeout .

30
00:02:11,141 --> 00:02:11,834
Every match,

31
00:02:11,834 --> 00:02:13,453
with its file name in front.

32
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:16,517
That is how you find which config mentions a setting.

33
00:02:18,567 --> 00:02:24,348
Text tools clean up lists. Type: sort fruits.txt. Sort puts the lines in order.

34
00:02:24,898 --> 00:02:26,325
To count repeats,

35
00:02:26,325 --> 00:02:27,247
sort first,

36
00:02:27,247 --> 00:02:29,680
then pipe into unique dash c.

37
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:32,365
Type: sort fruits.txt | uniq -c.

38
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:38,184
Unique only collapses neighbours, so sorting first matters. Apple appears three times.

39
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:42,277
Biggest count first. Add a pipe: | sort -rn.

40
00:02:43,227 --> 00:02:50,438
Now a table. Type: cut -d, -f1 users.csv. Cut takes one column.

41
00:02:50,988 --> 00:02:56,364
Dash d sets the divider, a comma. Dash f picks the field. Field one is the name.

42
00:02:58,414 --> 00:03:06,179
To back up or ship a folder, pack it with tar. Type: tar -czf backup.tar.gz project.

43
00:03:06,729 --> 00:03:07,559
C creates,

44
00:03:07,559 --> 00:03:08,639
Z compresses,

45
00:03:08,639 --> 00:03:10,050
F names the file.

46
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:12,042
No output means success.

47
00:03:12,042 --> 00:03:14,367
Check the size with: ls -lh.

48
00:03:14,917 --> 00:03:23,002
Look inside without unpacking. Type: tar -tzf backup.tar.gz. T means table of contents.

49
00:03:23,552 --> 00:03:26,901
Restore it somewhere safe. Type: mkdir restore.

50
00:03:27,451 --> 00:03:36,859
Then: tar -xzf backup.tar.gz -C restore. X extracts, capital C sets the target folder.

51
00:03:37,409 --> 00:03:40,268
Check with: ls restore. The project is back.

52
00:03:42,318 --> 00:03:43,641
Four myths to clear up.

53
00:03:44,191 --> 00:03:49,460
Myth one: one arrow adds to a file. No. One arrow replaces it. Two arrows add.

54
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:52,875
Myth two: a pipe saves output to a file.

55
00:03:52,875 --> 00:03:53,090
No.

56
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:55,239
A pipe feeds the next command.

57
00:03:55,239 --> 00:03:56,815
An arrow feeds a file.

58
00:03:57,365 --> 00:04:02,016
Myth three: grep changes the file. No. It only reads, and prints matches.

59
00:04:02,566 --> 00:04:07,515
Myth four: tar compresses by itself. It only packs. The Z flag compresses.

60
00:04:08,065 --> 00:04:10,156
Arrow to file. Pipe to command.

61
00:04:12,206 --> 00:04:14,531
Here is how you check your own understanding.

62
00:04:15,081 --> 00:04:17,961
Redirect output into a file, and append to it.

63
00:04:18,511 --> 00:04:20,175
Chain two commands with a pipe.

64
00:04:20,725 --> 00:04:22,944
Search with grep, using at least two flags.

65
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:24,945
Sort and count repeats.

66
00:04:25,495 --> 00:04:27,287
Pack and unpack a tar archive.

67
00:04:27,837 --> 00:04:29,415
Say what each tar flag does.

68
00:04:29,965 --> 00:04:31,587
To pass, you need all six.

69
00:04:33,637 --> 00:04:37,946
Your turn, in your virtual machine. Work only inside a practice folder.

70
00:04:38,496 --> 00:04:44,000
One: save the output of L S slash etc into a file, then append the date to it.

71
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:47,153
Two: use a pipe to count the lines in that file.

72
00:04:47,703 --> 00:04:52,674
Three: write a small log with a few ERROR lines, then use grep to count and number them.

73
00:04:53,224 --> 00:04:57,106
Four: make a list with repeats, and show a count of each, biggest first.

74
00:04:57,656 --> 00:05:02,520
Five: pack a folder with tar, list the archive, and restore it into a new folder.

75
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:06,059
Six: compare the restored folder with the original,

76
00:05:06,059 --> 00:05:08,638
and write one sentence about what you found.

77
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:12,495
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

78
00:05:28,045 --> 00:05:28,920
Quick check.

79
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:31,411
Which arrow adds to the end of a file?

80
00:05:31,961 --> 00:05:33,220
What does a pipe connect?

81
00:05:33,770 --> 00:05:35,860
Which grep flag counts matching lines?

82
00:05:36,410 --> 00:05:37,968
Which tar flag extracts?

83
00:05:38,518 --> 00:05:45,067
Objective covered: MC04-O03.

84
00:05:45,617 --> 00:05:47,686
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

85
00:05:58,236 --> 00:06:01,628
Next lesson: users, permissions and S S H keys.

86
00:06:02,178 --> 00:06:05,506
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
